(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["aitareya"]={"slug":"aitareya","name":"Aitareya","deva":"ऐतरेय","note":"","intro":["A principal (mukhya) Upaniṣad of the Ṛgveda, forming Adhyāyas 4–6 of the second Āraṇyaka of the Aitareya Āraṇyaka (i.e. Aitareya Āraṇyaka 2.4–2.6), itself belonging to the Aitareya Brāhmaṇa of the Śākala recension. It is one of the ten Upaniṣads commented on by Ādi Śaṅkara. Tradition ascribes the Aitareya corpus to the sage Mahidāsa Aitareya (\"son of Itarā\"). Compact but pivotal, it moves from a grand cosmogony to the great Ṛgvedic mahā-vākya: प्रज्ञानं ब्रह्म — <em>prajñānaṃ brahma</em>, \"Consciousness is Brahman.\"","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (aitareya.itx, encoded by Sunder Hattangadi, proofread with John Manetta). Accent-marked aitareyopaniShatsasvarA.itx and Śaṅkara's Aitareyopaniṣad-bhāṣya consulted for word-division. The original text is in the public domain; all transliteration, glosses, translations, and commentary digests below are this compiler's own work."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Aitareya Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["ऐतरेय उपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad of (Mahidāsa) Aitareya\"","A principal (mukhya) Upaniṣad of the Ṛgveda, forming Adhyāyas 4–6 of the second Āraṇyaka of the Aitareya Āraṇyaka (i.e. Aitareya Āraṇyaka 2.4–2.6), itself belonging to the Aitareya Brāhmaṇa of the Śākala recension. It is one of the ten Upaniṣads commented on by Ādi Śaṅkara. Tradition ascribes the Aitareya corpus to the sage Mahidāsa Aitareya (\"son of Itarā\"). Compact but pivotal, it moves from a grand cosmogony to the great Ṛgvedic mahā-vākya: प्रज्ञानं ब्रह्म — <em>prajñānaṃ brahma</em>, \"Consciousness is Brahman.\"","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (aitareya.itx, encoded by Sunder Hattangadi, proofread with John Manetta). Accent-marked aitareyopaniShatsasvarA.itx and Śaṅkara's Aitareyopaniṣad-bhāṣya consulted for word-division. The original text is in the public domain; all transliteration, glosses, translations, and commentary digests below are this compiler's own work."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Structure of the whole Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Adhyāya</td><td>Khaṇḍas</td><td>Theme</td><td>Core movement</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>3</td><td>Cosmogony (sṛṣṭi)</td><td>The Self alone is; it creates the worlds, the world-guardian deities, the cosmic Person, and food; then enters its own creation.</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>1</td><td>The three births (trīṇi janmāni)</td><td>The self's continuity through seed, child, and rebirth; Vāmadeva's liberation-from-the-womb.</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>1</td><td>Consciousness is Brahman</td><td>\"Who is this Self?\" → the synonyms of prajñāna → prajñānaṃ brahma; the knower's immortality.</td></tr></table>","Following your series, each unit is given as Devanāgarī → IAST → pada/key-term gloss → literal → paraphrase → visual → practical steps → 5-commentary digest."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. Ādi Śaṅkara (c. 700 CE) — Aitareyopaniṣad-bhāṣya, the foundational Advaita commentary. Reads the entire cosmogony as a teaching device (adhyāropa–apavāda): creation is narrated only to lead the seeker, by stages, to the non-dual identity of the indwelling consciousness with Brahman declared in prajñānaṃ brahma. Vāmadeva's case proves jīvan-mukti through knowledge.","2. Madhvācārya / Ānanda Tīrtha (13th c.) — Aitareyopaniṣad-bhāṣya, the Dvaita reading. The Ātman of mantra 1 is Viṣṇu-Hari, the one independent reality; creation is real and by His will; jīvas and matter are eternally distinct and dependent. Prajñānaṃ brahma names the Lord as the conscious controller in whom all dependent consciousness is grounded. (Replaces Sureśvara, who wrote no Aitareya commentary.)","3. Rangarāmānuja (17th c.) — Prakāśikā, the Viśiṣṭādvaita reading. Brahman is Nārāyaṇa, the Self of all, with sentient and insentient reality as His body (śarīra). Creation is real; the Lord enters as antaryāmin; the jīva is a distinct-yet-inseparable mode. The closing ascent is the released soul's attainment of likeness to the Lord.","4. The Advaita-Ashrama reading-tradition (Swami Gambhīrānanda's standard literal rendering &amp; class-notes; here in this compiler's paraphrase) — the careful translator's voice: settles the anvaya, decodes the cryptic anatomy (the suture vidṛti, the three dwelling-places), the etymology Idandra → Indra, and the precise sense of each prajñāna-synonym.","5. Sri Aurobindo (early 20th c.; The Upanishads and related notes; here in this compiler's paraphrase) — the integral / psychological-evolutionary reading. The cosmogony is the descent of the one Consciousness into form; the four worlds are planes of being, the Puruṣa's organs are powers; the three births figure the soul's evolution; prajñānaṃ brahma is the seer's discovery that all existence is a self-deployment of consciousness — matter's secret and evolution's goal.","On usage: under each unit, Śaṅkara gives the Advaita construal; Madhva the Dvaita (theistic-pluralist) alternative; Rangarāmānuja the Viśiṣṭādvaita; Gambhīrānanda-tradition the literal settling of sense; Aurobindo the evolutionary-psychological angle. Digests are in this compiler's words, not verbatim quotations."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Ṛgvedic peace invocation)","title":"","sa":["वाङ् मे मनसि प्रतिष्ठिता मनो मे वाचि प्रतिष्ठितम् । आविरावीर्म एधि ।","वेदस्य म आणीस्थः । श्रुतं मे मा प्रहासीः ।","अनेनाधीतेनाहोरात्रान्संदधामि । ऋतं वदिष्यामि । सत्यं वदिष्यामि ।","तन्मामवतु । तद्वक्तारमवतु । अवतु माम् । अवतु वक्तारम् ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["vāṅ me manasi pratiṣṭhitā mano me vāci pratiṣṭhitam | āvir āvīr ma edhi |","vedasya ma āṇīsthaḥ | śrutaṃ me mā prahāsīḥ |","anenādhītenāhorātrān sandadhāmi | ṛtaṃ vadiṣyāmi | satyaṃ vadiṣyāmi |","tan mām avatu | tad vaktāram avatu | avatu mām | avatu vaktāram | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[["vāk me manasi pratiṣṭhitā","f. nom. + loc. + pp.","my speech is established in my mind"],["manaḥ me vāci pratiṣṭhitam","n. nom. + loc.","my mind is established in my speech"],["āviḥ āvīḥ me edhi","adv. (intensive) + impv. of edh","O self-luminous one, become manifest to me"],["vedasya me āṇīsthaḥ","m. gen. + nom.","be the fastening-peg (bearer) of the Veda for me"],["śrutam me mā prahāsīḥ","n. acc. + prohib. aor.","let not what I have heard desert me"],["anena adhītena","n. instr. sg.","by this (study) studied"],["ahorātrān sandadhāmi","m. acc. pl. + 1 sg.","I join (the) days and nights"],["ṛtam · satyam vadiṣyāmi","n. acc. + 1 sg. fut.","I shall speak the right · the true"],["tat mām avatu","pron. + impv.","may that protect me"]],"lit":"\"May my speech be established in my mind; may my mind be established in my speech. O self-luminous one, become manifest to me. Be for me the bearer of the Veda; let not what I have heard forsake me. By this study I join days and nights together. I shall speak the right; I shall speak the true. May that protect me; may that protect the teacher; may it protect me; may it protect the teacher. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"The Ṛgvedic opening prayer asks for the mutual rooting of mind and speech (so that what is thought is spoken and what is spoken is meant), for retention of learning, and for ceaseless study \"joining day and night.\" As in the Taittirīya, the seeker vows to speak ṛta and satya and asks protection for both pupil and teacher.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the mutual establishment of speech and mind removes the two great obstacles to study — forgetting and misexpression; \"self-luminous one\" is the ātman/Veda-light invoked to reveal itself."],["Madhva","the light prayed to is Hari, the bestower of memory and meaning; retention of the Veda is by His grace, not mere effort."],["Rangarāmānuja","the antaryāmin is asked to manifest as the inner controller of both speech and mind, harmonising the instruments for revelation."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","glosses āṇīsthaḥ as \"be the linch-pin/fastener (that holds the Veda together for me)\" and ahorātrān sandadhāmi as \"I unite the days and nights (in continuous study).\""],["Sri Aurobindo","mind and word are the two poles of the expressive consciousness; their mutual founding is the condition for the mantra — truth-speech that carries the reality it names."]],"notes":["Visual — mind ⇄ speech, and the vow","<table><tr><td>Asks for</td><td>So that</td></tr><tr><td>speech rooted in mind · mind rooted in speech</td><td>thought and word never diverge</td></tr><tr><td>āvir edhi (self-luminous, reveal yourself)</td><td>the inner light discloses meaning</td></tr><tr><td>vedasya āṇīsthaḥ · śrutaṃ mā prahāsīḥ</td><td>the Veda is held fast; learning is not lost</td></tr><tr><td>ahorātrān sandadhāmi</td><td>study is continuous, day and night</td></tr><tr><td>ṛtaṃ · satyaṃ vadiṣyāmi</td><td>cognition (ṛta) and speech (satya) are bound to truth</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Recite before study: it sets the single most practical aim of learning — that mind and mouth agree, and that what is heard is retained.","•  \"I join day and night\" is a charge to continuity of practice — not bursts, but an unbroken thread.","•  The same ṛta/satya vow as the Taittirīya marks a shared Vedic discipline: align thought and speech with truth before seeking knowledge."]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya I — The Cosmogony","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya I","title":"The Cosmogony","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The first chapter narrates creation in three movements: the Self resolves and makes the worlds (khaṇḍa 1), shapes the cosmic Person and the world-guardian deities who then need an abode (khaṇḍa 2), and creates food and finally enters its own creation through the crown of the head — discovering itself as Brahman (khaṇḍa 3). Śaṅkara reads the whole as adhyāropa (deliberate superimposition) whose purpose is the later apavāda (retraction) into pure consciousness."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Adhyāya I, Khaṇḍa 1","title":"The Self creates the worlds and the cosmic Person","sa":["ॐ आत्मा वा इदमेक एवाग्र आसीत् । नान्यत्किञ्चन मिषत् ।","स ईक्षत लोकान्नु सृजा इति ॥ १ ॥","स इमाँल्लोकानसृजत । अम्भो मरीचीर्मरमापः ।","अदोऽम्भः परेण दिवं द्यौः प्रतिष्ठा । अन्तरिक्षं मरीचयः ।","पृथिवी मरो या अधस्तात्ता आपः ॥ २ ॥","स ईक्षतेमे नु लोका लोकपालान्नु सृजा इति ।","सोऽद्भ्य एव पुरुषं समुद्धृत्यामूर्छयत् ॥ ३ ॥","तमभ्यतपत् । तस्याभितप्तस्य मुखं निरभिद्यत यथाण्डम् ।","मुखाद्वाग्वाचोऽग्निर्नासिके निरभिद्येतां नासिकाभ्यां प्राणः ।","प्राणाद्वायुरक्षिणी निरभिद्येतामक्षीभ्यां चक्षुश्चक्षुष आदित्यः ।","कर्णौ निरभिद्येतां कर्णाभ्यां श्रोत्रं श्रोत्राद्दिशस्त्वङ्निरभिद्यत","त्वचो लोमानि लोमभ्य ओषधिवनस्पतयो हृदयं निरभिद्यत","हृदयान्मनो मनसश्चन्द्रमा नाभिर्निरभिद्यत नाभ्या अपानोऽपानान्मृत्युः","शिश्नं निरभिद्यत शिश्नाद्रेतो रेतस आपः ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ ātmā vā idam eka evāgra āsīt | nānyat kiñcana miṣat | sa īkṣata lokān nu sṛjā iti || 1 ||","sa imāṃl lokān asṛjata | ambho marīcīr maram āpaḥ | ado'mbhaḥ pareṇa divaṃ dyauḥ pratiṣṭhā | antarikṣaṃ marīcayaḥ | pṛthivī maro yā adhastāt tā āpaḥ || 2 ||","sa īkṣateme nu lokā lokapālān nu sṛjā iti | so'dbhya eva puruṣaṃ samuddhṛtyāmūrchayat || 3 ||","tam abhyatapat | tasyābhitaptasya mukhaṃ nirabhidyata yathāṇḍam | mukhād vāk | vāco'gniḥ |","nāsike nirabhidyetām | nāsikābhyāṃ prāṇaḥ | prāṇād vāyuḥ | ... hṛdayān manaḥ | manasaś candramāḥ |","nābhyā apānaḥ | apānān mṛtyuḥ | śiśnād retaḥ | retasa āpaḥ || 4 ||"],"words":[["ātmā vā idam eka eva agra āsīt","m. nom. + n. + indecl. + impf.","the Self alone was this, one only, in the beginning"],["na anyat kiñcana miṣat","n. nom. + pres.part. of miṣ","nothing else whatever was winking (active)"],["īkṣata","3 sg. impf. mid. of īkṣ","(he) reflected / saw"],["lokān nu sṛjai","m. acc. pl. + 1 sg. subj.","\"let me create the worlds\""],["ambhaḥ · marīcayaḥ · maraḥ · āpaḥ","n./f./m./f.","the water-heaven · the rays (sky) · the mortal (earth) · the waters (below)"],["pareṇa divam","adv. + acc.","beyond heaven"],["pratiṣṭhā","f. nom. sg.","support, foundation"],["lokapālān","m. acc. pl.","world-guardians (presiding deities)"],["adbhyaḥ puruṣaṃ samuddhṛtya","f. abl. + m. acc. + ger.","having drawn a Person up from the waters"],["amūrchayat","3 sg. impf. caus. of mūrch","gave (him) form / solidified"],["abhyatapat","3 sg. impf. of abhi-tap","brooded over / heated (with tapas)"],["mukhaṃ nirabhidyata yathāṇḍam","n. nom. + 3 sg. impf. pass. + adv.","the mouth burst open like an egg"],["mukhād vāk · vāco'gniḥ","abl. + nom.","from the mouth, speech; from speech, fire"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. The Self alone was this, one only, in the beginning. Nothing else whatever was active. He reflected: 'Let me create the worlds.' He created these worlds: ambhas (the water above heaven), marīci (the sky), mara (the mortal earth), āpaḥ (the waters below) — that ambhas is beyond heaven, heaven its support; the mid-region is the marīcis; the earth is mara; what is below, those are the waters. He reflected: 'Here are the worlds; let me create their guardians.' From the very waters he drew up a Person and gave him form. He brooded over him; from him thus brooded over, the mouth burst open like an egg; from the mouth, speech; from speech, fire. The nostrils burst open… from breath, air… from the heart, mind; from mind, the moon… from the navel, the down-breath; from the down-breath, death… from the generative organ, semen; from semen, the waters.\"","para":"Before anything, only Ātman is — no second thing even \"winks.\" By a mere resolve of seeing (īkṣaṇa), the Self projects four worlds, then raises a cosmic Puruṣa from the waters and, \"brooding\" on him, lets his organs split open one by one, each yielding a faculty and from it a cosmic deity (mouth→speech→fire; nostrils→breath→air; eyes→sight→sun; ears→hearing→quarters; skin→hairs→plants; heart→mind→moon; navel→apāna→death; organ→semen→water). The macrocosm is born as the anatomy of a single Person.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"Self alone, one only\" establishes the advaya thesis at the outset; the creation is recounted (adhyāropa) only to be withdrawn later into pure consciousness. Īkṣata (he saw) proves the cause is conscious, refuting an unconscious pradhāna."],["Madhva","the Ātman is Viṣṇu, really creating real worlds by His independent will; the Puruṣa and deities are His genuine creatures, eternally distinct from and dependent on Him."],["Rangarāmānuja","Brahman-Nārāyaṇa, having sentient/insentient reality as His body, really projects the worlds and ensouls the cosmic Person as antaryāmin; the emanation displays His ordering of His own body."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","fixes the four worlds (the supra-celestial waters ambhas, the sky marīci, the earth mara, the nether waters), and renders amūrchayat as \"consolidated/gave a compact form.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","the lone Self's īkṣaṇa is the original creative gaze; the worlds are planes and the Puruṣa the universal form whose opening \"doors\" are the cosmic powers — consciousness deploying itself as world."]],"notes":["Visual — the organ → faculty → deity emanation","<table><tr><td>Organ (of the cosmic Puruṣa)</td><td>Faculty</td><td>Cosmic deity</td></tr><tr><td>mouth</td><td>speech (vāk)</td><td>fire (agni)</td></tr><tr><td>nostrils</td><td>breath (prāṇa)</td><td>air (vāyu)</td></tr><tr><td>eyes</td><td>sight (cakṣus)</td><td>sun (āditya)</td></tr><tr><td>ears</td><td>hearing (śrotra)</td><td>quarters (diśaḥ)</td></tr><tr><td>skin</td><td>hairs (lomāni)</td><td>herbs &amp; trees</td></tr><tr><td>heart</td><td>mind (manas)</td><td>moon (candramā)</td></tr><tr><td>navel</td><td>down-breath (apāna)</td><td>death (mṛtyu)</td></tr><tr><td>generative organ</td><td>semen (retas)</td><td>waters (āpaḥ)</td></tr></table>","<pre>   ĀTMĀ (one, alone)  ── &quot;let me create&quot; (īkṣaṇa)</pre>","│","four worlds:  ambhas · marīci · mara · āpaḥ","│","PURUṢA raised from the waters, given form, \"brooded\" (tapas)","│","organs burst open  →  faculties  →  cosmic deities  (see table)","Practical steps / observations","•  The verb is īkṣata (\"he saw/reflected\") — creation begins not with a tool but with seeing/intention; a contemplative cue that the world is, at root, a movement of consciousness.","•  Trace the organ→deity column as a body-cosmos meditation: each of your faculties has a cosmic counterpart, preparing the later teaching that the same consciousness pervades both.","•  Note \"nothing else winked\" — a precise pointer for stilling: imagine the state before any second thing stirs."]},{"ref":"Adhyāya I, Khaṇḍa 2","title":"The deities seek a home; the body is built","sa":["ता एता देवताः सृष्टा अस्मिन्महत्यर्णवे प्रापतन् ।","तमशनापिपासाभ्यामन्ववार्जत् । ता एनमब्रुवन्नायतनं नः प्रजानीहि","यस्मिन्प्रतिष्ठिता अन्नमदामेति ॥ १ ॥","ताभ्यो गामानयत्ता अब्रुवन्न वै नोऽयमलमिति ।","ताभ्योऽश्वमानयत्ता अब्रुवन्न वै नोऽयमलमिति ॥ २ ॥","ताभ्यः पुरुषमानयत्ता अब्रुवन्सुकृतं बतेति पुरुषो वाव सुकृतम् ।","ता अब्रवीद्यथायतनं प्रविशतेति ॥ ३ ॥","अग्निर्वाग्भूत्वा मुखं प्राविशद्वायुः प्राणो भूत्वा नासिके प्राविशत्","आदित्यश्चक्षुर्भूत्वाक्षिणी प्राविशाद्दिशः श्रोत्रं भूत्वा कर्णौ प्राविशन्","ओषधिवनस्पतयो लोमानि भूत्वा त्वचं प्राविशन्श्चन्द्रमा मनो भूत्वा हृदयं प्राविशत्","मृत्युरपानो भूत्वा नाभिं प्राविशदापो रेतो भूत्वा शिश्नं प्राविशन् ॥ ४ ॥","तमशनायापिपासे अब्रूतामावाभ्यामभिप्रजानीहीति ।","ते अब्रवीदेतास्वेव वां देवतास्वाभजाम्येतासु भागिन्न्यौ करोमीति ।","तस्माद्यस्यै कस्यै च देवतायै हविर्गृह्यते भागिन्यावेवास्यामशनायापिपासे भवतः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["tā etā devatāḥ sṛṣṭā asmin mahaty arṇave prāpatan | tam aśanāyāpipāsābhyām anvavārjat |","tā enam abruvann āyatanaṃ naḥ prajānīhi yasmin pratiṣṭhitā annam adāmeti || 1 ||","tābhyo gām ānayat | tā abruvan na vai no'yam alam iti | tābhyo'śvam ānayat | ... na vai no'yam alam iti || 2 ||","tābhyaḥ puruṣam ānayat | tā abruvan sukṛtaṃ bateti | puruṣo vāva sukṛtam | tā abravīd yathāyatanaṃ praviśateti || 3 ||","agnir vāg bhūtvā mukhaṃ prāviśat | vāyuḥ prāṇo bhūtvā nāsike prāviśat | ... candramā mano bhūtvā hṛdayaṃ prāviśat | mṛtyur apāno bhūtvā nābhiṃ prāviśat | āpo reto bhūtvā śiśnaṃ prāviśan || 4 ||","tam aśanāyāpipāse abrūtām āvābhyām abhiprajānīhīti | te abravīd etāsv eva vāṃ devatāsv ābhajāmy etāsu bhāginyau karomīti |","tasmād yasyai kasyai ca devatāyai havir gṛhyate bhāginyāv evāsyām aśanāyāpipāse bhavataḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["mahati arṇave prāpatan","m. loc. + 3 pl. impf.","fell into the great ocean (of saṃsāra)"],["aśanāyā-pipāsābhyām anvavārjat","f. instr. du. + 3 sg. impf.","afflicted (him) with hunger and thirst"],["āyatanaṃ naḥ prajānīhi","n. acc. + impv.","\"find out an abode for us\""],["yasmin pratiṣṭhitāḥ annam adāma","loc. + 1 pl. subj.","\"established in which we may eat food\""],["gām · aśvam · puruṣam ānayat","m. acc. + 3 sg.","he brought a cow · horse · human"],["na vai naḥ ayam alam","indecl. + adj.","\"this is not enough for us\""],["sukṛtam bata","n. + interj.","\"well made, indeed!\""],["yathāyatanaṃ praviśata","adv. + impv. pl.","\"enter each your respective abode\""],["agniḥ vāk bhūtvā mukhaṃ prāviśat","nom. + ger. + 3 sg.","fire, becoming speech, entered the mouth"],["abhiprajānīhi","impv.","\"find (a place) for (us two) as well\""],["etāsu devatāsu ābhajāmi","f. loc. pl. + 1 sg.","\"I give you a share in these very deities\""],["bhāginyau karomi","f. acc. du. + 1 sg.","\"I make you two sharers\""],["haviḥ gṛhyate","n. nom. + 3 sg. pass.","an oblation is taken/offered"]],"lit":"\"These deities, (thus) created, fell into this great ocean. He afflicted him (the Person) with hunger and thirst. They said to him: 'Find us an abode established in which we may eat food.' He brought them a cow; they said, 'This is not enough for us.' He brought them a horse; they said, 'This is not enough.' He brought them a human; they said, 'Well made, indeed!' — the human is verily the well-made (vessel). He said to them: 'Enter, each into your own station.' Fire, becoming speech, entered the mouth; air, becoming breath, the nostrils; the sun, becoming sight, the eyes; the quarters, becoming hearing, the ears; herbs and trees, becoming hairs, the skin; the moon, becoming mind, the heart; death, becoming the down-breath, the navel; the waters, becoming semen, the generative organ. Then hunger and thirst said: 'Find an abode for us two also.' He said to them: 'I assign you a share in these very deities; I make you two sharers in them.' Therefore, to whatever deity an oblation is offered, hunger and thirst are sharers in it.\"","para":"The newly-made cosmic powers, cast into the ocean of becoming, are stricken by hunger and thirst and beg for a stable dwelling \"in which to eat.\" A cow and a horse are offered and rejected; only the human body is accepted — sukṛtam, \"well made.\" The deities now re-enter the body as its faculties (the reverse of khaṇḍa 1: the cosmos folds back into the person). Hunger and thirst, left out, are made co-sharers in every deity — which is why every oblation feeds them too.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the rejection of cow and horse and acceptance of the human shows the human body uniquely fit for jñāna; the deities entering as faculties prepares the question \"who, then, is the indweller behind them?\" answered in khaṇḍa 3."],["Madhva","the powers are real dependent deities placed by Hari in the body; their hunger marks the jīva's eternal dependence; the Lord alone is nir-apekṣa (without need)."],["Rangarāmānuja","the body is the Lord's ordered abode for the jīva and the presiding deities, all of whom form His body; hunger/thirst as sharers shows the interdependence within His cosmic frame."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies anvavārjat (\"afflicted/pursued with\"), alam (\"adequate/enough\"), and that \"the human is sukṛta\" means the well-fashioned receptacle for the deities; notes the deliberate reversal of khaṇḍa 1."],["Sri Aurobindo","the powers plunge into the \"sea\" of material existence and need an organised vehicle; only the human form can hold the full company of cosmic energies — the body as the meeting-place of the gods."]],"notes":["Visual — the cosmic powers re-enter the body","<table><tr><td>Cosmic power</td><td>becomes</td><td>enters</td></tr><tr><td>fire</td><td>speech</td><td>the mouth</td></tr><tr><td>air</td><td>breath</td><td>the nostrils</td></tr><tr><td>sun</td><td>sight</td><td>the eyes</td></tr><tr><td>quarters</td><td>hearing</td><td>the ears</td></tr><tr><td>herbs &amp; trees</td><td>hairs</td><td>the skin</td></tr><tr><td>moon</td><td>mind</td><td>the heart</td></tr><tr><td>death</td><td>down-breath (apāna)</td><td>the navel</td></tr><tr><td>waters</td><td>semen</td><td>the generative organ</td></tr></table>","Rejected vessels: cow → \"not enough\" · horse → \"not enough\" · human → \"well made!\" (the only fit abode for the indwelling powers).","Practical steps / observations","•  The body is declared sukṛta — \"well made,\" the chosen seat of the divine powers; a basis for treating the body as a sacred instrument, not a thing to despise (anticipating Vallī-III food-ethics elsewhere).","•  Hunger and thirst as co-sharers in every deity is a sober teaching: appetite is woven into every faculty; it is to be honoured (fed) and mastered, not denied.","•  Watch the mirror-symmetry with khaṇḍa 1 (organs→deities, then deities→organs): a contemplation of how cosmos and body are the same set of powers read outward and inward."]},{"ref":"Adhyāya I, Khaṇḍa 3","title":"Food, the Self's entry, and the name \"Indra\"","sa":["स ईक्षतेमे नु लोकाश्च लोकपालाश्चान्नमेभ्यः सृजा इति ॥ १ ॥","सोऽपोऽभ्यतपत्ताभ्योऽभितप्ताभ्यो मूर्तिरजायत ।","या वै सा मूर्तिरजायतान्नं वै तत् ॥ २ ॥","तदेनत्सृष्टं पराङ्त्यजिघांसत्तद्वाचाजिघृक्षत्तन्नाशक्नोद्वाचा ग्रहीतुम् ... (3–9)","तदपानेनाजिघृक्षत्तदावयत्सैषोऽन्नस्य ग्रहो यद्वायुरनायुर्वा एष यद्वायुः ॥ १० ॥","स ईक्षत कथं न्विदं मदृते स्यादिति । स ईक्षत कतरेण प्रपद्या इति ।","स ईक्षत यदि वाचाभिव्याहृतं यदि प्राणेनाभिप्राणितं ... अथ कोऽहमिति ॥ ११ ॥","स एतमेव सीमानं विदर्यैतया द्वारा प्रापद्यत । सैषा विदृतिर्नाम द्वास्तदेतन्नान्दनम् ।","तस्य त्रय आवसथास्त्रयः स्वप्ना अयमावसथोऽयमावसथोऽयमावसथ इति ॥ १२ ॥","स जातो भूतान्यभिव्यैख्यत्किमिहान्यं वावदिषदिति । स एतमेव पुरुषं ब्रह्म ततममपश्यत् ।","इदमदर्शमिती ३ ॥ १३ ॥","तस्मादिदन्द्रो नामेदन्द्रो ह वै नाम । तमिदन्द्रं सन्तमिन्द्र इत्याचक्षते परोक्षेण ।","परोक्षप्रिया इव हि देवाः परोक्षप्रिया इव हि देवाः ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["sa īkṣateme nu lokāś ca lokapālāś cānnam ebhyaḥ sṛjā iti || 1 ||","so'po'bhyatapat | tābhyo'bhitaptābhyo mūrtir ajāyata | yā vai sā mūrtir ajāyatānnaṃ vai tat || 2 ||","tad enat sṛṣṭaṃ parāṅ tyajighāṃsat | tad vācājighṛkṣat | tan nāśaknod vācā grahītum ... (faculties 3–9) ...","tad apānenājighṛkṣat | tad āvayat | saiṣo'nnasya graho yad vāyuḥ | anāyur vā eṣa yad vāyuḥ || 10 ||","sa īkṣata kathaṃ nv idaṃ mad-ṛte syād iti | sa īkṣata katareṇa prapadyā iti | ... atha ko'ham iti || 11 ||","sa etam eva sīmānaṃ vidāryaitayā dvārā prāpadyata | saiṣā vidṛtir nāma dvāḥ | tad etan nāndanam |","tasya traya āvasathās trayaḥ svapnāḥ | ayam āvasatho'yam āvasatho'yam āvasatha iti || 12 ||","sa jāto bhūtāny abhivyaikhyat | kim ihānyaṃ vāvadiṣad iti | sa etam eva puruṣaṃ brahma tatamam apaśyat | idam adarśam iti || 13 ||","tasmād idandro nāma | idandro ha vai nāma | tam idandraṃ santam indra ity ācakṣate parokṣeṇa | parokṣapriyā iva hi devāḥ || 14 ||"],"words":[["annam ebhyaḥ sṛjai","n. acc. + dat. + 1 sg. subj.","\"let me create food for these\""],["apaḥ abhyatapat","f. acc. + 3 sg.","he brooded over the waters"],["mūrtiḥ ajāyata · annaṃ vai tat","f. nom. + 3 sg. + n.","a form was born; that (form) is verily food"],["parāṅ tyajighāṃsat","adv. + 3 sg. desid.","(the food) sought to flee away"],["vācā ajighṛkṣat","f. instr. + 3 sg. desid.","(he) wished to seize it by speech"],["na aśaknot grahītum","3 sg. impf. + inf.","was not able to grasp (it)"],["apānena āvayat","m. instr. + 3 sg.","by the down-breath he grasped (ate) it"],["eṣaḥ annasya grahaḥ yad vāyuḥ","m. nom.","this air/apāna is the seizer of food"],["anāyuḥ vā eṣaḥ yad vāyuḥ","adj.","this vāyu is the very life (without which none lives)"],["kathaṃ nu idaṃ mad-ṛte syāt","interrog. + abl. + opt.","\"how could this exist without me?\""],["katareṇa prapadye","instr. + 1 sg. subj.","\"by which (door) shall I enter?\""],["sīmānaṃ vidārya","m. acc. + ger. of vi-dṝ","having split open the suture (of the skull)"],["vidṛtiḥ nāma dvāḥ","f. nom. + n. + f.","the door named vidṛti (the cleft)"],["nāndanam","n. nom. sg.","the place of delight"],["trayaḥ āvasathāḥ · trayaḥ svapnāḥ","m. nom. pl.","three dwelling-places · three dream(-states)"],["brahma tatamam apaśyat","n. + superl. + 3 sg.","saw (it as) the all-pervading Brahman"],["idam adarśam","pron. + 1 sg. aor. of dṛś","\"this I have seen\""],["idandraḥ → indraḥ","m. nom.","\"It-seer / I-saw-this\" → \"Indra\" (cryptic)"],["parokṣa-priyāḥ iva hi devāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the gods are, as it were, fond of the indirect"]],"lit":"\"He reflected: 'Here are the worlds and their guardians; let me create food for them.' He brooded over the waters; from the waters thus brooded over, a form was born — and that form is verily food. The created food sought to flee. He wished to seize it by speech — could not grasp it by speech… (so too by breath, eye, ear, skin, mind, generative organ — each failed). He wished to seize it by the down-breath (apāna) — and grasped it. This air (apāna) is the seizer of food. He reflected: 'How could this (body) exist without me?' He reflected: 'By which door shall I enter?' He reflected: 'If speaking is by speech, breathing by breath… then who am I?' Having split open this very suture (of the skull), he entered by that door. This is the door called vidṛti; this is the place of delight (nāndana). It has three dwelling-places, three dream-states: 'this abode, this abode, this abode.' Being born, he looked upon (all) beings: 'What else here would one call other (than the Self)?' He saw this very Person as the all-pervading Brahman: 'This I have seen.' Therefore he is named Idandra ('It/this-I-saw'); 'Idandra' is indeed his name. Him who is Idandra they call 'Indra' cryptically — for the gods, as it were, love the indirect; the gods love the indirect indeed.\"","para":"The Self creates food (the form born of the brooded waters) — but food \"flees,\" and none of the outward faculties can hold it; only apāna (the digestive, downward breath) can. Then comes the decisive turn: the Self, asking \"how could this body live without me — and who, then, am I?\", enters its own creation through the crown-suture (vidṛti), taking up three abodes (waking-eye, dream-mind, deep-sleep-heart). Born into the body, it looks around and recognises the indwelling Person as the all-pervading Brahman — \"this I have seen\" (idam adarśam) — and is therefore secretly named Idandra, which the gods, \"fond of the indirect,\" contract to Indra.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the climax of the chapter — the Self \"enters\" not by motion but by appearing as the indweller (jīva); vidṛti and the three abodes are the suṣumnā-crown and the three states; idam adarśam → Idandra → Indra teaches that the supreme is named indirectly. The food-section shows even the body's life depends on the Self."],["Madhva","the indweller is the Lord (Hari) as antaryāmin, distinct from the jīva who also dwells there; \"Indra\" names the supreme Lord; the soul's recognition is of its dependence on Him, not identity with Him."],["Rangarāmānuja","the Self entering \"by the door of the head\" is Brahman-Nārāyaṇa pervading His bodily creation as inner ruler; \"saw Brahman as all-pervading\" affirms His vyāpti; the jīva is His mode, awakened to His indwelling."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","decodes vidṛti (the cranial suture/brahma-randhra), the three āvasathas as the loci of the three states, āvayat (\"ate/assimilated\"), and the playful etymology Idandra → Indra with parokṣa-priya (\"the gods like indirect names\")."],["Sri Aurobindo","food is matter that \"flees\" until seized by the vital breath; the Self's entry \"through the head\" is consciousness descending into and inhabiting its form; the recognition \"this I have seen\" is the soul's awakening to itself as the indwelling Spirit — named indirectly because the Divine works behind a veil."]],"notes":["Visual — three movements of Khaṇḍa 3","(A) FOOD          waters brooded → \"mūrti\" = anna; food flees;","speech·breath·eye·ear·skin·mind·organ all FAIL to seize it","<pre>                                     └──&gt; only APĀNA grasps it</pre>","(\"the seizer of food is vāyu\")","(B) ENTRY         \"How could this body be, without me?  Who am I?\"","<pre>                   Self splits the skull-suture  ── door = VIDṚTI (nāndana)</pre>","takes THREE abodes / three dream-states:","eye (waking) · mind (dream) · heart (deep sleep)","(C) RECOGNITION   born, it beholds all beings → sees the indwelling","Puruṣa as the all-pervading BRAHMAN  →  \"idam adarśam\"","<pre>                       → name: IDANDRA  ── (gods love the cryptic) →  INDRA</pre>","<table><tr><td>The three abodes (āvasatha)</td><td>State</td></tr><tr><td>the (right) eye</td><td>waking</td></tr><tr><td>the mind (dream-space)</td><td>dream</td></tr><tr><td>the heart</td><td>deep sleep</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Apāna alone seizes food: a precise physiological-contemplative note — assimilation belongs to the downward breath; meditative traditions take this as a cue for digestion and prāṇa-awareness.","•  The pivotal question is \"atha ko'ham?\" — \"then who am I?\": when every function is assigned to a faculty, the residue is the witness. Use it as a direct self-enquiry: subtract each function and ask what remains.","•  The three āvasathas map onto the avasthā-traya (waking/dream/sleep) of Māṇḍūkya — a portable framework for watching the one Self persist across the three states.","•  \"The gods love the indirect\": a teaching about why truth is veiled in names and symbols — the cryptic Idandra→Indra invites unpacking, not passive acceptance."]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya II — The Three Births of the Self","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya II (single khaṇḍa)","title":"Seed, child, rebirth; Vāmadeva's freedom","sa":["ॐ पुरुषे ह वा अयमादितो गर्भो भवति यदेतद्रेतः ।","तदेतत्सर्वेभ्योऽङ्गेभ्यस्तेजः संभूतमात्मन्येवात्मानं बिभर्ति","तद्यदा स्त्रियां सिञ्चत्यथैनज्जनयति तदस्य प्रथमं जन्म ॥ १ ॥","तत्स्त्रिया आत्मभूयं गच्छति यथा स्वमङ्गं तथा । तस्मादेनां न हिनस्ति ।","साऽस्यैतमात्मानमत्र गतं भावयति ॥ २ ॥","सा भावयित्री भावयितव्या भवति । तं स्त्री गर्भं बिभर्ति । सोऽग्र एव","कुमारं जन्मनोऽग्रेऽधिभावयति । स यत्कुमारं जन्मनोऽग्रेऽधिभावयत्यात्मानमेव","तद्भावयत्येषां लोकानां सन्तत्यै । एवं सन्तता हीमे लोकास्तदस्य द्वितीयं जन्म ॥ ३ ॥","सोऽस्यायमात्मा पुण्येभ्यः कर्मभ्यः प्रतिधीयते । अथास्यायमितर आत्मा","कृतकृत्यो वयोगतः प्रैति । स इतः प्रयन्नेव पुनर्जायते तदस्य तृतीयं जन्म ॥ ४ ॥","तदुक्तमृषिणा गर्भे नु सन्नन्वेषामवेदमहं देवानां जनिमानि विश्वा ।","शतं मा पुर आयसीररक्षन्नधः श्येनो जवसा निरदीयमिति ।","गर्भ एवैतच्छयानो वामदेव एवमुवाच ॥ ५ ॥","स एवं विद्वानस्माच्छरीरभेदादूर्ध्व उत्क्रम्यामुष्मिन्स्वर्गे लोके","सर्वान्कामानाप्त्वामृतः समभवत्समभवत् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ puruṣe ha vā ayam ādito garbho bhavati yad etad retaḥ |","tad etat sarvebhyo'ṅgebhyas tejaḥ sambhūtam ātmany evātmānaṃ bibharti |","tad yadā striyāṃ siñcaty athainaj janayati | tad asya prathamaṃ janma || 1 ||","tat striyā ātmabhūyaṃ gacchati yathā svam aṅgaṃ tathā | tasmād enāṃ na hinasti | sāsyaitam ātmānam atra gataṃ bhāvayati || 2 ||","sā bhāvayitrī bhāvayitavyā bhavati | ... tad asya dvitīyaṃ janma || 3 ||","so'syāyam ātmā puṇyebhyaḥ karmabhyaḥ pratidhīyate | athāsyāyam itara ātmā kṛtakṛtyo vayogataḥ praiti | sa itaḥ prayann eva punar jāyate | tad asya tṛtīyaṃ janma || 4 ||","tad uktam ṛṣiṇā — garbhe nu sann anv eṣām avedam ahaṃ devānāṃ janimāni viśvā | śataṃ mā pura āyasīr arakṣann adhaḥ śyeno javasā niradīyam iti | garbha evaitac chayāno vāmadeva evam uvāca || 5 ||","sa evaṃ vidvān asmāc charīra-bhedād ūrdhva utkramyāmuṣmin svarge loke sarvān kāmān āptvāmṛtaḥ samabhavat | samabhavat || 6 ||"],"words":[["puruṣe ādito garbhaḥ bhavati","m. loc. + adv. + nom.","in man this (self) first becomes a germ"],["yat etat retaḥ","n. nom.","namely, this semen"],["sarvebhyaḥ aṅgebhyaḥ tejaḥ sambhūtam","n. abl. pl. + nom.","the vigour gathered from all the limbs"],["ātmani eva ātmānaṃ bibharti","m. loc. + acc. + 3 sg.","he bears the self within his own self"],["striyāṃ siñcati · janayati","f. loc. + 3 sg.","pours into a woman · gives it birth"],["prathamaṃ janma","n. nom. sg.","the first birth"],["striyāḥ ātma-bhūyaṃ gacchati","f. gen. + n. acc.","becomes one with the woman's self"],["na hinasti · bhāvayati","3 sg.","does not harm (her) · nourishes"],["santatyai","f. dat. sg.","for the continuance (of the worlds)"],["dvitīyaṃ janma","n. nom. sg.","the second birth (the child)"],["puṇyebhyaḥ karmabhyaḥ pratidhīyate","n. dat. pl. + 3 sg. pass.","is appointed (in his stead) for holy works"],["kṛtakṛtyaḥ vayogataḥ praiti","m. nom. + 3 sg.","having done his duty, aged, he departs"],["itaḥ prayan punaḥ jāyate","pres.part. + 3 sg.","departing hence, is born again"],["tṛtīyaṃ janma","n. nom. sg.","the third birth"],["garbhe nu san avedam","m. loc. + pres.part. + 1 sg. aor.","while yet in the womb, I knew"],["devānāṃ janimāni viśvā","m. gen. pl. + n. acc. pl.","all the births of the gods"],["śataṃ puraḥ āyasīḥ arakṣan","f. acc. pl. + 3 pl.","a hundred iron forts guarded (held) me"],["śyenaḥ javasā niradīyam","m. nom. + n. instr. + 1 sg.","(as) a hawk, with speed, I flew forth"],["amṛtaḥ samabhavat","m. nom. + 3 sg.","he became immortal"]],"lit":"\"In man this (self) first becomes a germ — the semen, which is the vigour gathered from all the limbs; he bears the self within his own self. When he pours it into a woman, then he brings it forth — that is its first birth. It becomes one with the woman's self, as if her own limb; therefore it does not harm her, and she nourishes this self of his come into her… The woman bears the embryo; (the father) nourishes the child before and after birth; in nourishing the child he nourishes his own self, for the continuance of these worlds — that is its second birth. This self of his (the son) is set in his place for holy works; then his other self (the father), his task done and grown old, departs; departing hence he is born again — that is its third birth. So the seer (Vāmadeva) declared: 'While yet in the womb I knew all the births of these gods; a hundred iron forts held me down — (yet) as a hawk, with speed, I burst forth.' So spoke Vāmadeva, even as he lay in the womb. He, knowing thus, ascending upward at the body's dissolution, attained all desires in that heavenly world and became immortal — became immortal.\"","para":"The Self's continuity is told as three births: (1) as seed — the father's own essence borne within him and poured forth; (2) as the child — the father reborn in the son, whom he nourishes \"for the continuance of the worlds\"; (3) as rebirth — the father, his duties handed to the son, departs and is born again. Against this chain of births stands Vāmadeva, who, knowing the Self even in the womb, broke through \"a hundred iron forts\" (the bonds of embodiment) \"like a hawk\" and, at death, became immortal — the Upaniṣad's proof that knowledge liberates.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the three births describe ordinary saṃsāra (continuity by progeny and rebirth); Vāmadeva's verse, cited from the Ṛgveda, exemplifies jīvan-mukti by vidyā — the knower transcends the very chain just described. \"Immortal\" = identity with the deathless Self, not a heaven of duration."],["Madhva","the births are real transmigration governed by karma under Hari's control; Vāmadeva's liberation is by the Lord's grace and knowledge of Him; \"immortality\" is the soul's released, Lord-dependent bliss, ranked by the soul's intrinsic capacity."],["Rangarāmānuja","progeny continues the embodied soul's worldly course; the knower, by upāsanā of the indwelling Lord, attains release — ascending by the arcirādi path to enjoy the Lord, \"all desires fulfilled\" in His presence."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies ātmabhūyaṃ gacchati (\"becomes part of her very self\"), pratidhīyate (\"is installed in his stead\"), and renders Vāmadeva's archaic verse (the iron citadels, the hawk) with the womb-knowledge reading."],["Sri Aurobindo","the three births are the soul's evolutionary persistence through heredity; Vāmadeva figures the awakened soul that, recognising its divinity even in the bondage of the embryo, breaks free of Nature's \"forts\" — the spirit outsoaring its material confinement."]],"notes":["Visual — the three births and the way out","<table><tr><td>Birth</td><td>As</td><td>Mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>First</td><td>seed (retas)</td><td>the father's gathered vigour, poured into the mother</td></tr><tr><td>Second</td><td>the child</td><td>the father reborn in the son; nourished \"for the world's continuance\"</td></tr><tr><td>Third</td><td>rebirth</td><td>the father, duties done, departs and is born again</td></tr></table>","...birth → birth → birth...   (the chain of saṃsāra, continued by progeny)","╳","VĀMADEVA: knew the Self \"while still in the womb\"","→ broke the \"hundred iron forts\" like a hawk (javasā niradīyam)","→ at death, ascended → all desires attained → AMṚTA (immortal)","Practical steps / observations","•  The \"three births\" reframe family life as the self perpetuating itself — a sober, non-sentimental view of progeny as the worlds' continuance (santati).","•  Vāmadeva is the hinge: liberation comes not by more births but by knowledge that cuts the chain — \"even in the womb.\" Use it as encouragement that realisation is a matter of insight, not circumstance.","•  The \"hundred iron forts\" is a vivid image for the body-mind's conditioning; the \"hawk's speed\" for the suddenness of release once knowledge dawns — contemplate which \"forts\" hold you."]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya III — Consciousness is Brahman","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya III (single khaṇḍa)","title":"\"Who is this Self?\" → prajñānaṃ brahma","sa":["ॐ कोऽयमात्मेति वयमुपास्महे कतरः स आत्मा । येन वा पश्यति येन","वा शृणोति येन वा गन्धानाजिघ्रति येन वा वाचं व्याकरोति येन","वा स्वादु चास्वादु च विजानाति ॥ १ ॥","यदेतद्धृदयं मनश्चैतत् । संज्ञानमाज्ञानं विज्ञानं प्रज्ञानं मेधा","दृष्टिर्धृतिर्मतिर्मनीषा जूतिः स्मृतिः सङ्कल्पः क्रतुरसुः कामो वश इति ।","सर्वाण्येवैतानि प्रज्ञानस्य नामधेयानि भवन्ति ॥ २ ॥","एष ब्रह्मैष इन्द्र एष प्रजापतिरेते सर्वे देवा इमानि च पञ्चमहाभूतानि","पृथिवी वायुराकाश आपो ज्योतींषीत्येतानीमानि च क्षुद्रमिश्राणीव ।","बीजानीतराणि चेतराणि चाण्डजानि च जारुजानि च स्वेदजानि चोद्भिज्जानि च","अश्वा गावः पुरुषा हस्तिनो यत्किञ्चेदं प्राणि जङ्गमं च पतत्रि च यच्च","स्थावरं सर्वं तत्प्रज्ञानेत्रं प्रज्ञाने प्रतिष्ठितं प्रज्ञानेत्रो लोकः","प्रज्ञा प्रतिष्ठा प्रज्ञानं ब्रह्म ॥ ३ ॥","स एतेन प्राज्ञेनात्मनास्माल्लोकादुत्क्रम्यामुष्मिन्स्वर्गे लोके","सर्वान्कामानाप्त्वामृतः समभवत्समभवत् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ ko'yam ātmeti vayam upāsmahe | kataraḥ sa ātmā | yena vā paśyati yena vā śṛṇoti yena vā gandhān ājighrati yena vā vācaṃ vyākaroti yena vā svādu cāsvādu ca vijānāti || 1 ||","yad etad dhṛdayaṃ manaś caitat | saṃjñānam ājñānaṃ vijñānaṃ prajñānaṃ medhā dṛṣṭir dhṛtir matir manīṣā jūtiḥ smṛtiḥ saṃkalpaḥ kratur asuḥ kāmo vaśa iti | sarvāṇy evaitāni prajñānasya nāmadheyāni bhavanti || 2 ||","eṣa brahmaiṣa indra eṣa prajāpatir ete sarve devā imāni ca pañca mahābhūtāni pṛthivī vāyur ākāśa āpo jyotīṃṣīty etāni... yat kiñcedaṃ prāṇi jaṅgamaṃ ca patatri ca yac ca sthāvaraṃ sarvaṃ tat prajñānetraṃ prajñāne pratiṣṭhitam | prajñānetro lokaḥ | prajñā pratiṣṭhā | prajñānaṃ brahma || 3 ||","sa etena prājñenātmanāsmāl lokād utkramyāmuṣmin svarge loke sarvān kāmān āptvāmṛtaḥ samabhavat | samabhavat || 4 ||"],"words":[["kaḥ ayam ātmā iti upāsmahe","interrog. + 1 pl. mid.","\"who is this Self?\" — (thus) we worship"],["kataraḥ saḥ ātmā","interrog.","which (of the two/many) is that Self?"],["yena paśyati / śṛṇoti / ājighrati","instr. + 3 sg.","that by which one sees / hears / smells"],["vācaṃ vyākaroti","f. acc. + 3 sg.","articulates speech"],["svādu ca asvādu ca vijānāti","n. acc. + 3 sg.","discerns the sweet and the unsweet"],["hṛdayaṃ manaḥ ca etat","n. nom.","this heart and this mind (are that Self)"],["saṃjñāna…vaśa","n./f./m. nom. (16 terms)","perception, command, discernment, knowledge, intelligence, sight, steadiness, thought, reflection, impulse, memory, resolve, will, life, desire, control"],["prajñānasya nāmadheyāni","n. gen. + nom. pl.","names of prajñāna (consciousness)"],["eṣaḥ brahmā · indraḥ · prajāpatiḥ","m. nom.","this is Brahmā · Indra · Prajāpati"],["pañca mahābhūtāni","n. nom. pl.","the five great elements"],["aṇḍa-/jāru-/sveda-/udbhij-jāni","n. nom. pl.","born of egg / womb / sweat / sprout"],["prajñā-netram · prajñāne pratiṣṭhitam","n. nom.","led by consciousness · founded on consciousness"],["prajñā pratiṣṭhā","f. nom.","consciousness is the foundation"],["prajñānaṃ brahma","n. nom.","Consciousness is Brahman"],["prājñena ātmanā utkramya","m. instr. + ger.","by the conscious Self, having ascended"],["amṛtaḥ samabhavat","m. nom. + 3 sg.","became immortal"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. 'Who is this Self?' — thus we meditate. Which is that Self? — that by which one sees, or hears, or smells odours, or articulates speech, or discerns the sweet and the unsweet. That which is this heart and this mind — (that is the Self). Perception, command, discernment, knowledge, intelligence, sight, steadfastness, thought, reflection, impulse, memory, resolve, will, vitality, desire, control — all these are but names of consciousness (prajñāna). This (consciousness) is Brahmā, is Indra, is Prajāpati, all these gods, and the five great elements — earth, air, space, water, light; these and those mingled with the minute, the seeds of every kind — born of egg, of womb, of sweat, of sprout; horses, cattle, men, elephants; whatever breathes, moving or flying, and whatever is stationary — all that is led by consciousness, founded on consciousness. The world is led by consciousness; consciousness is its foundation. Consciousness is Brahman. He, by this conscious Self, ascending from this world, attained all desires in that heavenly world and became immortal — became immortal.\"","para":"The whole Upaniṣad converges here. It opens with a question — \"Who is this Self we worship?\" — and answers that the Self is that by which all perceiving and knowing happens. The dozen-plus mental terms (perception, memory, will, desire…) are unmasked as so many names of one consciousness (prajñāna). That consciousness is then shown to underlie everything — gods, elements, every kind of creature, the moving and the still: all is led by and grounded in consciousness. Hence the great declaration: प्रज्ञानं ब्रह्म — Consciousness is Brahman. Whoever knows this, by that very conscious Self, becomes immortal.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the consummation of the text — prajñāna is pure, self-luminous consciousness, identical with Brahman and with the inmost Self; the many psychological terms are its vṛttis (modes), not separate entities. \"Consciousness is Brahman\" is a statement of identity (the jñāna-mahāvākya), and the knower is immediately liberated, not merely heaven-bound."],["Madhva","prajñāna is Viṣṇu, the supremely conscious Lord who leads (prajñā-netram) all beings as their inner controller; the jīva's consciousness is real but dependent, a finite reflection. \"Consciousness is Brahman\" identifies the Lord as the conscious ground, not the soul as identical with Him."],["Rangarāmānuja","Brahman is the substrate-consciousness in whom all sentient and insentient reality is established as His body; prajñānaṃ brahma affirms that the Lord, essentially knowledge, is the support of all — the jīva a conscious mode, distinct yet inseparable; the ascent is to the Lord's abode."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","renders each of the sixteen synonyms precisely (e.g. saṃjñāna \"perception/consciousness,\" ājñāna \"ordering/lordship,\" kratu \"purpose,\" asu \"vitality,\" vaśa \"desire/control\") and stresses that \"all are names of prajñāna\" makes the plurality collapse into one knowing principle; prajñā-netram = \"having consciousness for its guide.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","the seer's culminating insight that all existence is a deployment of consciousness — matter, life and mind are forms of one Cit; prajñānaṃ brahma is the charter of his evolutionary vision, in which consciousness is both the secret already present in things and the divinity toward which they evolve."]],"notes":["Visual — from the question to the mahā-vākya","\"ko'yam ātmā?\"  (who is the Self we worship?)","│","that BY WHICH one sees · hears · smells · speaks · tastes","│","<pre>   the many mental names ───────────────────────────────────┐</pre>","saṃjñāna · ājñāna · vijñāna · prajñāna · medhā · dṛṣṭi ·  │ all are","dhṛti · mati · manīṣā · jūti · smṛti · saṃkalpa · kratu ·  │ names of","asu · kāma · vaśa                                          │ PRAJÑĀNA","<pre>        │ ◄───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>","all gods · five elements · all births (egg/womb/sweat/sprout) ·","all beings (moving · flying · stationary)","│  \"prajñā-netram · prajñāne pratiṣṭhitam\"","▼","★  PRAJÑĀNAM BRAHMA  — Consciousness is Brahman  ★","│","knower → ascends → all desires attained → AMṚTA (immortal)","Practical steps / observations","•  This is the Aitareya's mahā-vākya, the Ṛgveda's contribution to the four great sentences (with tat tvam asi, ahaṃ brahmāsmi, ayam ātmā brahma). Hold it as a meditation seed: every act of knowing is Brahman in operation.","•  The method is subtraction-then-recognition: list the mental functions, see they are all one consciousness wearing many names, then recognise that same consciousness as the ground of all that exists.","•  Practical self-enquiry: instead of asking \"what do I know?\", ask \"what is the knowing by which I know anything?\" — that turn of attention is the whole teaching.","•  The doubled samabhavat (\"became immortal, became immortal\") marks finality — the Upaniṣad ends not in a state to reach but in a recognition that completes."]},{"ref":"Closing Śānti","title":"","sa":["ॐ वाङ् मे मनसि प्रतिष्ठिता ... तद्वक्तारमवतु ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ vāṅ me manasi pratiṣṭhitā ... tad vaktāram avatu | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"The Upaniṣad closes by repeating its opening Ṛgvedic invocation, returning mind and speech to their mutual rest and sealing the study with the threefold peace — ādhyātmika, ādhibhautika, ādhidaivika.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Thematic outline","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Unit</td><td>Title</td><td>Type</td><td>Key idea</td></tr><tr><td>Śānti</td><td>mind ⇄ speech</td><td>invocation</td><td>root speech in mind &amp; mind in speech; retain learning; speak truth</td></tr><tr><td>I.1</td><td>the worlds &amp; the Puruṣa</td><td>cosmogony</td><td>the lone Self sees and creates four worlds and the cosmic Person; organs → faculties → deities</td></tr><tr><td>I.2</td><td>the deities' home</td><td>cosmogony</td><td>the powers fall into the \"ocean,\" need an abode; only the human body fits; they re-enter as faculties</td></tr><tr><td>I.3</td><td>food, entry, \"Indra\"</td><td>cosmogony</td><td>apāna alone seizes food; the Self enters via vidṛti, takes 3 abodes; sees itself as Brahman → Idandra → Indra</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>the three births</td><td>continuity</td><td>seed → child → rebirth; Vāmadeva breaks free by knowledge even in the womb → immortal</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>prajñānaṃ brahma</td><td>mahā-vākya</td><td>the Self is that by which all is known; all mental names are one consciousness; Consciousness is Brahman</td></tr><tr><td>Śānti</td><td>closing peace</td><td>invocation</td><td>the opening prayer repeated; threefold peace</td></tr></table>","Arc of the Upaniṣad: cosmos out of the Self (Adhyāya I) → the Self's continuity and the knower's escape (Adhyāya II) → the Self recognised as the consciousness that is Brahman (Adhyāya III). The grand outward narration of creation exists only to turn the seeker inward, to the prajñāna by which the whole was ever known."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Notes on this document","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- Sanskrit follows the Ṛgveda (Aitareya) recension as in sanskritdocuments.org aitareya.itx, cross-checked against the accented aitareyopaniShatsasvarA.itx. Chapter/section numbering follows the standard Adhyāya–Khaṇḍa division used in Śaṅkara's bhāṣya (= Aitareya Āraṇyaka 2.4–2.6).","- IAST is given for every unit; gloss tables give full pada analysis for the short/verse units and key-term analysis for the long prose passages, where exhaustive particle-by-particle glossing would obscure more than it reveals.","- The five-commentary digests are this compiler's summaries of each tradition's characteristic reading — Śaṅkara, Madhva, Rangarāmānuja from their Sanskrit works, and the Gambhīrānanda reading-tradition and Sri Aurobindo in paraphrase. They are not verbatim translations of any modern copyrighted edition.","- Commentary set adapted from the Taittirīya instalment: Madhva replaces Sureśvara, since Sureśvara wrote no vārttika on the Aitareya, whereas Madhva's Aitareyopaniṣad-bhāṣya is extant — this also adds the Dvaita voice to the Advaita (Śaṅkara) and Viśiṣṭādvaita (Rangarāmānuja) readings."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
